.\" elispall(1) — comprehensive all-in-one man page for elisprs
.\" Modeled after zshall(1): every man page in one document.
.\" Render with:  man -l man/man1/elispall.1
.\" Install:      cp man/man1/elispall.1 /usr/local/share/man/man1/
.TH ELISPALL 1 "2026-09-26" "elisprs 0.1.17" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
elispall \- the elisprs comprehensive reference (invocation, reader, special
forms, standard library, architecture)
.SH OVERVIEW
This is the all-in-one
.B elisprs
reference. For the concise command page see
.BR elisp (1).
.PP
.B elisprs
runs Emacs Lisp (\fB.el\fR) as standalone programs. It is built to become the
fifth language hosted on the
.B fusevm
bytecode VM, after
.BR zshrs ", " stryke ", " awkrs ", and " vimlrs .
Each form is macroexpanded and lowered to fusevm bytecode, which fusevm runs
(with its JIT tiers), calling back into the elisprs object heap.
.SH INVOCATION
.B elisp
[\fIOPTIONS\fR] [\fIFILE\fR | \fB\-e\fR \fIPROGRAM\fR]
.PP
See
.BR elisp (1)
for the full option list:
.BR \-e / \-\-eval ,
.BR \-\-lsp ,
.BR \-\-dap ,
.BR \-\-aot ,
.BR \-V / \-\-version ,
.BR \-h / \-\-help .
With no arguments a REPL is started (parenthesis-balanced multi-line input;
Ctrl-D exits).
.SH "DESIGN: REUSE / OWN SPLIT"
Emacs Lisp is a Lisp-2 (separate value and function cells per symbol) and is, by
default, dynamically scoped. No general-purpose embeddable Lisp provides those,
so elisprs splits the problem:
.TP
.B Own \(em the data model
Cons cells, symbols, strings, vectors, closures, buffers and the rest live on
the elisprs heap (\fIsrc/host.rs\fR) as \fBObj\fR values addressed by fusevm
\fBValue\fR handles.
.TP
.B Own \(em the reader
.I src/reader.rs
is an elisp-correct reader: \fB1+\fR/\fB1\-\fR are symbols, and \fB#'\fR,
\fB?c\fR, dotted pairs, vectors, records and backquote read as in Emacs.
.TP
.B Own \(em the semantics
The obarray (value + function cells), dynamic and lexical binding, the special
forms, the compiler to fusevm bytecode, and the standard library are all
in-crate.
.SH "READER SYNTAX"
Integers, floats, strings with escapes, symbols (including \fB1+\fR, \fB1\-\fR,
\fB<=\fR, \fB:keywords\fR), \fBnil\fR/\fBt\fR, \fB'\fRquote, \fB#'\fRfunction,
\fB?c\fR character literals, \fB;\fR comments, dotted pairs, vectors, and
backquote (\fB`\fR, \fB,\fR, \fB,@\fR, read as \fB(\e` X)\fR, \fB(\e, X)\fR,
\fB(\e,@ X)\fR).
.SH "SPECIAL FORMS"
.B quote function lambda progn prog1 if when unless cond and or while setq let
.B let* defun defmacro defvar defconst condition-case unwind-protect
.PP
Lambda lists accept \fB&optional\fR and \fB&rest\fR. Macros expand and are
re-evaluated.
.B condition-case
matches the \fBerror\fR umbrella and specific error symbols.
.SH "STANDARD LIBRARY (A SELECTION)"
.TP
.B Arithmetic
+ \- * / % mod 1+ 1\- abs max min = /= < > <= >=
.TP
.B Lists
car cdr cons list append nth nthcdr reverse length member memq assoc assq
.TP
.B Predicates
eq eql equal null not numberp integerp floatp stringp symbolp consp listp atom
functionp
.TP
.B Symbols / cells
set symbol-value symbol-function fset boundp fboundp symbol-name intern
make-symbol
.TP
.B Strings
concat string= string-equal string< upcase downcase number-to-string
string-to-number
.TP
.B I/O and format
format message princ prin1 print terpri
.TP
.B Functional
funcall apply mapcar mapc identity
.SH EXECUTION
Each top-level form is macroexpanded and lowered by \fIsrc/compiler.rs\fR to a
\fBfusevm::Chunk\fR; lambda bodies become sub-chunks. Elisp operations run
through an \fBOp::Extended\fR handler that calls back into the heap, while core
arithmetic and comparison lower to native fusevm ops, so fusevm's Cranelift
JIT tiers and the
.B \-\-aot
path (\fBfusevm::aot\fR) apply to them.
.SH LIMITATIONS
Known divergences from GNU Emacs are recorded in \fIBUGS.md\fR in the source
tree. No display: buffers exist, windows and frames do not.
.SH "SEE ALSO"
